# TypeScript Types Rules

## Type Definition Structure
- Define entity types with proper interfaces or type aliases.
- Use Zod schemas for validation and type inference.
- Keep type definitions organized by entity or domain.
- Export types from a central location for each entity.

## Type Definition Pattern
```typescript
// types/entity.ts
import { z } from 'zod';
import type { Database } from './supabase';

// Base Zod schema
export const entitySchema = z.object({
  name: z.string().min(2, 'Name must be at least 2 characters'),
  code: z.string().min(2).max(50),
  description: z.string().optional(),
  isActive: z.boolean().default(true),
  // Other fields
});

// TypeScript types from Zod schema
export type CreateEntityDto = z.infer<typeof entitySchema>;

// Entity with database fields
export type Entity = CreateEntityDto & {
  id: string;
  created_at: string;
  updated_at: string;
};

// Database type from Supabase schema
export type EntityRecord = Database['public']['Tables']['entities']['Row'];

// Filter types for queries
export interface EntityFilters {
  search?: string;
  isActive?: boolean;
  page?: number;
  limit?: number;
  sortBy?: string;
  sortDirection?: 'asc' | 'desc';
}

// Response types
export interface EntityListResponse {
  data: Entity[];
  metadata: {
    total: number;
    page: number;
    limit: number;
    totalPages: number;
  };
}
```

## Supabase Types
- Generate and use Supabase types from the database schema.
- Keep generated types separate from application types.
- Use proper type mapping between Supabase and application types.

## Supabase Type Generation
```typescript
// types/supabase.ts
export type Json =
  | string
  | number
  | boolean
  | null
  | { [key: string]: Json | undefined }
  | Json[];

export interface Database {
  public: {
    Tables: {
      entities: {
        Row: {
          id: string;
          name: string;
          code: string;
          description: string | null;
          is_active: boolean;
          created_at: string;
          updated_at: string;
        };
        Insert: {
          id?: string;
          name: string;
          code: string;
          description?: string | null;
          is_active?: boolean;
          created_at?: string;
          updated_at?: string;
        };
        Update: {
          id?: string;
          name?: string;
          code?: string;
          description?: string | null;
          is_active?: boolean;
          created_at?: string;
          updated_at?: string;
        };
      };
      // Other tables
    };
    // Views, Functions, etc.
  };
}
```

## Use of Zod
- Use Zod for runtime validation and TypeScript type generation.
- Create consistent validation schema patterns.
- Define reusable schema fragments for common patterns.
- Implement proper error handling for validation errors.

## Type Utilities
- Create utility types for common patterns.
- Use TypeScript utility types (Partial, Omit, Pick, etc.) appropriately.
- Define discriminated unions for state management.
- Use generic types for reusable components and functions.

## Type Utility Examples
```typescript
// types/utils.ts
export type AsyncState<T> = 
  | { status: 'idle' }
  | { status: 'loading' }
  | { status: 'success'; data: T }
  | { status: 'error'; error: Error };

export type Paginated<T> = {
  data: T[];
  metadata: {
    total: number;
    page: number;
    limit: number;
    totalPages: number;
  };
};

export type WithId<T> = T & { id: string };

export type FormState<T> = {
  values: T;
  errors: Partial<Record<keyof T, string>>;
  touched: Partial<Record<keyof T, boolean>>;
  isSubmitting: boolean;
  isValid: boolean;
};
```

## Type Safety
- Enable strict TypeScript checking in tsconfig.json.
- Avoid using `any` type; use `unknown` when type is uncertain.
- Use proper type narrowing with type guards.
- Define proper return types for all functions.
- Use exhaustive type checking for switch statements.

## Custom Type Declarations
- Create declaration files for third-party libraries that lack types.
- Extend existing type declarations when needed.
- Use proper module augmentation for global types.

## API Response Types
- Define consistent response type structures.
- Use proper error response types.
- Type API endpoints with request and response types.